    "summary": "Half-Step Tower opener, monster St. of Circumstance, fiery Jerry",
    "blurb": "The set opens with a soaring Mississippi Half‑Step into Franklin’s Tower and tight El Paso/Mexicali Blues. The second set erupts with a monster Saint Of Circumstance jam, a blistering Miracle > Bertha sequence and a fiery Casey Jones finale.",
    "review": "Nassau Coliseum on November 2, 1979 delivered one of the late‑’70s high‑water marks for the Dead. The night launches with Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo soaring straight into Franklin’s Tower, setting an immediate lift that carries through a crisp El Paso and a polka‑spiced Mexicali Blues. Lazy Lightning rolls into Supplication with Phil’s manic, driving bass line, while Brent’s keyboards add bright Rhodes textures that push the band forward.\n\nThe second set is where the show truly shines: I Need A Miracle segues flawlessly into Bertha, then bursts through Lost Sailor into a towering Saint Of Circumstance jam that expands into an expansive Space and drums segment. Jerry is on fire throughout—his lead work on Miracle, Not Fade Away and the Half‑Step Tower is described as “monumental arena rock,” while his witty pre‑Casey banter adds the usual Dead humor. Brent’s synth‑rich fills dominate the jam, and Mickey’s drumspace weirdness keeps the rhythmic tide flowing. The night closes with a classic Casey Jones that captures both energy and lyrical swagger, making this a must‑listen performance.",
